Uncertainty Machine and its numerical methods — Lafarge and Possolo (@met-machine: 0, is #reference, #part-of @met-sources) { r_0JJ7DQ8KRD6ACMN16THF87F3PW
Thomas Lafarge and Antonio Possolo. NIST Uncertainty Machine and User’s Manual. [Official application](https://uncertainty.nist.gov/) · [Manual supplied by the application](https://uncertainty.nist.gov/NISTUncertaintyMachine-UserManual.pdf) · [Official background and instructions](https://uncertainty.nist.gov/about/about_English.md.html).

Read the complete manual, including input distributions, correlations, model expression, scalar and vector outputs, and the worked examples used in your investigation. Record application and manual versions separately: the inspected application identified version 1.6.4, while an available manual identifies version 1.4, November 2020.

Compare the implementation with Lafarge and Possolo, “The NIST Uncertainty Machine,” NCSLI Measure Journal of Measurement Science 10(3), 2015, pages 20–27, [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1080/19315775.2015.11721732). Use authorized access to the article; the freely readable official manual supports the required study.

The application is an optional independent numerical check. Preserve your model, distributions, correlations, settings, and results so a different implementation can reproduce them. NIST federal text and separately published article/software terms differ; inspect the actual notices before redistribution. Metadata and official access checked on 28 October 2026. No dependency on a live website is needed for the hand-derived benchmark cases.

**Return:** [Measurement and metrology sources — books, guides, and research](../Sources.lingua#r_6H584EJ2Z9D11PG89Y21Y8T1A3).
